---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Engineering leadership and applied AI</p>
      <h1>AI adoption for complex <span>engineering organisations.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">I have spent 20 years leading engineering teams and large delivery work. I am now applying that experience to AI adoption: what teams use, what leaders change, and how results are assessed.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/now">What I am working on</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/about">About</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="hero-status" aria-label="Current focus">
      <p class="status-label">[ CURRENT FOCUS ]</p>
      <ol>
        <li><span>01</span> Agent design and evaluation</li>
        <li><span>02</span> Workflow automation</li>
        <li><span>03</span> AI in engineering leadership</li>
      </ol>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="signal-section" aria-labelledby="direction-heading">
    <div class="section-label">01 / Direction</div>
    <div>
      <h2 id="direction-heading">From individual experiments to team practice.</h2>
      <p>Many engineering teams begin with individual AI experiments. That is a sensible starting point. The harder question is what should change in the delivery workflow, and who owns that change.</p>
      <p>My focus is the practical side: choosing a use case, giving teams room to try it, and keeping what works. The same questions appear in planning, requirements, design, testing and deployment.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="principles-section" aria-labelledby="principles-heading">
    <div class="section-heading"><p class="section-label">02 / Working principles</p><h2 id="principles-heading">What I look for in AI adoption.</h2></div>
    <div class="principles-grid">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Start with delivery</h3><p>Start with one task in the team’s normal work. Define the problem before choosing a tool.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Make practice shared</h3><p>If a prompt, workflow or specification helps, document it. One person’s shortcut is not a team practice.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Measure the result</h3><p>Ask what changed: time, quality, rework or decision-making. If the value is unclear, do not scale it.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="foundation-section" aria-labelledby="foundation-heading">
    <div><p class="section-label">03 / Current work</p><h2 id="foundation-heading">Where I am putting time now.</h2></div>
    <div><p>I am learning and building in three areas: agent design and evaluation, workflow automation, and AI-enabled engineering leadership.</p><p>I want to understand where these approaches help in real delivery work and where they add unnecessary complexity.</p><p>I will publish case studies when there is work worth showing. They will cover the decisions made, the limitations and the results.</p><a class="text-link" href="/now">What I am working on</a></div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
